  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 310 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nUpper ridging remains the dominant pattern across the western CONUS. \nThis will promote warm and dry conditions through the extended \nforecast. While the northern stream remains fairly active, attendant \nfrontal boundaries dropping into western Nebraska will largely be \nmoisture starved and not really herald any significant airmass \nchange until next weekend. Ensemble guidance remains bullish on the \ndry conditions with both EPS and GEFS output showing < 10% \nprobabilities for seeing a tenth of an inch or more anywhere in the \nforecast area through Thursday. With westerly flow in a drier \nairmass and influence from a thermal ridge across the central and \nsouthern Rockies, daily high temperatures will remain above normal. \nA look at LBF and VTN specifically shows highs generally at or above \nthe 75th percentile in each site's respective climatology. By the \nweekend, a pattern change appears possible as western ridging breaks \ndown and introduces a southwest flow. This occurs ahead of deeper \ntroughing that should settle into the Great Basin sometime \nSaturday/Sunday. Confidence in details remains low but overall an \nend to the dry/warm stretch appears probable for at least portions \nof the High Plains sometime around Day 7-8.",
